  "query_preview": "(Today through Saturday)...\nIssued at 246 AM EDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this morning shows high pressure east of the \nmiddle Atlantic States, allowing a warm and humid southerly flow \nacross Central Indiana. Water vapor shows a stream of Pacific \nmoisture pushing across the Rockies to a ridge axis over the upper \nmidwest before spilling southeast across Indiana and the eastern \nGreat Lakes. It was this stream of upper moisture that was providing \nmid and upper level cloud cover across Central Indiana and points \nnorth. HRRR profiles show plentiful dry air within the lower levels. \nDew points were creeping higher, into the 60s. \n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nThe models continue keep the stream of upper level Pacific moisture \nstreaming across a ridge axis over the Great Lakes, as upper level \nhigh pressure remains parked over TN/AR. A thermal ridge within the \nlower levels is suggested to set up over the upper midwest, as warm \nair advection continues across Indiana. Surface high pressure to the \neast looks to continue to control our lower levels, providing warmer \nand more humid flow. Still, forecast soundings today and tonight \nsuggest abundant dry air within the lower levels. Thus we will \nexpect a warmer day with ongoing warm air advection along with \npassing mid to high clouds through tonight.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...\n\nForecast soundings on both Monday and Tuesday continue to suggest \nplentiful dry air within the lower levels along with ongoing warm \nair advection still in place. 850mb temps are suggested to rise to \naround 22C, bringing very warm temperatures for early September. \nConvective temperatures in the 100s are suggested also, thus no CU. \nWithin the upper levels the strong upper level high pressure system \nwill begin to surge northeast somewhat, driving any forcing dynamics \nnorth, across the Great Lakes. Thus mostly clear skies will be \nexpected with hot high temperatures in the 90s. \n\nWednesday through Thursday...\n\nConfidence continues to be high for hot and humid weather at the end \nof this workweek continuing into next weekend.\n\nThe upper pattern on Wednesday into Thursday suggests strong upper \nlevel high pressure over the Tennessee and Ohio river valleys with \nthe upper flow streaming across the Great Lakes with some embedded \nwaves within that flow. A thermal ridge and frontal boundary looks \nto be draped across the Great Lakes at that time, keeping Indiana \nwithin the warm sector. Forecast soundings at this point show \nconvective temperatures in the mid 90s, which can be reached. Steep \nlapse rates are also suggested, thus isolated diurnal convection \ncannot be ruled out either. However the main story will be September \nheat with highs in the low to mid 90s and high heat index values. \nHeat Advisories will be possible in the week ahead.\n\nFriday and Saturday...\n\nThe ridging over the upper midwest is suggested to become sharper on \nby Friday and Saturday. This will allow for NW flow to develop across \nCentral Indiana, also introducing a possible pattern of placing us \non the periphery edge of a ring of fire. The previous warm front \nand thermal ridge to the north is suggested to sag southward across \nIndiana and this may cause an extra convection triggering feature. \nForecast soundings suggest plentiful CAPE with steep lapse rates on \nFriday and Saturday. Thus pops day will continued to be needed. \nCaveats include the frontal boundary sagging south of Central \nIndiana on Saturday, resulting in cooler northerly flow while \nintroducing subsidence within the column. Furthermore, possible \nclouds along the front may prevent max heating, keeping temperatures \ncooler. Overall, some pops should be included here with highs above \nseasonal normals.",
